{"name":"Shell White","hex":"#f0ebe0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard external covering of an animal.\nThe calcareous or chitinous external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other invertebrates.","The calcareous or chitinous external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other invertebrates."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove the outer covering or shell of something.","To bombard, to fire projectiles at, especially with artillery."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shell"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shell (projectile)","blocks":[{"text":"A shell, in a modern military context, is a projectile whose payload contains an explosive, incendiary, or other chemical filling. Originally it was called a bombshell, but \"shell\" has come to be unambiguous in a military context. A shell can hold a tracer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/WWI_shells.JPG/330px-WWI_shells.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shell_(projectile)"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg/500px-W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg/500px-W48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"US scientists with a full-scale cut-away model of the W48 155 millimeter nuclear artillery shell, a very small tactical nuclear weapon with an explosive yield equivalent to 72 tons of TNT (0.072 kiloton). It could be fired from any standard 155 mm (6.1 inch) howitzer (e.g., the M114 or M198).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AW48_155-millimeter_nuclear_shell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg/500px-USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg/1280px-USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg/500px-USMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"155mm M107 projectiles, with fuzes fitted.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSMC-100414-M-5241M-001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Ming_Dynasty_eruptor_proto-cannon.